{{also|aspersión}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|la|aspersiōnem}}, from {{m|la|aspersiō||sprinkling}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vininn126-aspersion.wav|Audio (US)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# An attack on somebody's [[reputation]] or good name, often in the phrase ''[[cast aspersions|to cast aspersions upon…]]''. {{defdate|from late 16th c.}}
#: {{syn|en|calumny|slander|Thesaurus:slander}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1799|year_published=1906|author=[[w:Pennsylvania House of Representatives|Pennsylvania House of Representatives]]|title=[[s:State Documents on Federal Relations|State Documents on Federal Relations]]|chapter=[[s:State Documents on Federal Relations/10|Resolutions of the House of Representatives of Pennsylvania to Kentucky]]|publisher=University of Pennsylvania|page=[[s:Page:State Documents on Federal Relations.djvu/35|21]]|passage=No criminality can be infered or punishment inflicted, but for writing, printing, uttering, or publishing false, scandalous and malicious '''aspersions''' against the government.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1911|author=Joseph Conrad|authorlink=Joseph Conrad|title=Under Western Eyes|url=http://www.gutenberg.org/files/2480/2480-h/2480-h.htm|passage=Even the comfort of the bottle might conceivably fail him in this supreme crisis. At such an age nothing but a halter could cure the pangs of an unquenchable passion. And, besides, there was the wild exasperation aroused by the unjust '''aspersions''' and the contumely of the house, with the maddening impossibility to account for that mysterious thrashing, added to these simple and bitter sorrows.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A [[sprinkling]], especially of [[holy water]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Tempest|4|1|text=If thou dost break her virgin knot before<brAll sanctimonious ceremonies may<br>With full and holy rite be minister'd,<br>No sweet '''aspersion''' shall the heavens let fall<br>To make this contract grow; but barren hate {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|in plural}} [[slander]], [[calumny]]

